.TH PCAPSIPDUMP 8 "2026-08-28" "pcapsipdump 1.2.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
pcapsipdump \- dump SIP sessions to one pcap file per call
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pcapsipdump
.RB [ \-fpUhV ]
.RB [ \-i
.IR interface " |"
.B \-r
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR template ]
.RB [ \-P
.IR pid_file ]
.RB [ \-v
.IR level ]
.RB [ \-R
.IR filter ]
.RB [ \-m
.IR regex ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR regex ]
.RB [ \-l
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-B
.IR size ]
.RB [ \-T
.IR seconds ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR trigger : action : parameter ]
.RI [ expression ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pcapsipdump
is a libpcap-based sniffer that records SIP signalling together with the
RTP/RTCP media belonging to it, in the same file format
.BR "tcpdump \-w"
produces. Instead of one large capture it writes a separate file for each SIP
session, even when thousands of calls are in progress at once.
.PP
The same binary also splits an existing bulk capture offline, which needs no
privileges:
.PP
.RS
.B pcapsipdump \-r bulk.pcap \-d /tmp/calls
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h
Show a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-V
Show the version and exit.
.TP
.BI \-i " interface"
Capture from the named interface.
.B any
captures from all of them.
.TP
.BI \-r " file"
Read from a
.B .pcap
file instead of an interface.
.TP
.BI \-d " template"
Output directory, or a file name template. See
.B FILE NAME TEMPLATES
below.
.TP
.B \-f
Stay in the foreground instead of forking and detaching from the controlling
terminal. This is what the shipped systemd unit uses.
.TP
.BI \-P " pid_file"
When forking, write the PID here. Defaults to
.IR /var/run/pcapsipdump.pid .
.TP
.B \-p
Do
.I not
put the interface into promiscuous mode.
.TP
.B \-U
Write packet-buffered. Slower, but a file that is still being written is always
consistent and can be opened at any time.
.TP
.BI \-v " level"
Verbosity; higher is noisier. Level 1 and above also print the libpcap version
and the effective options at start-up.
.TP
.BI \-B " size"
Kernel capture buffer size, expressed in bytes or with a quantifier:
.BR KB ", " KiB ", " MB ", " MiB ", " GB ", " GiB .
For example
.BR "\-B 64MiB" .
Raise this to a few MiB or more to stop the kernel dropping packets under load.
.TP
.BI \-R " filter"
What to record alongside the signalling:
.B rtp+rtcp
(the default),
.BR rtp ,
.BR rtpevent ,
.BR t38 ,
or
.BR none .
.TP
.BI \-m " regex"
Which SIP methods open a new capture file. Defaults to
.BR ^(INVITE|OPTIONS|REGISTER)$ .
.TP
.BI \-n " regex"
Record only calls to or from a number matching this extended regular
expression. See
.BR regex (7).
.TP
.BI \-l " N"
Sample the traffic: record only every
.IR N -th
call.
.TP
.BI \-T " seconds"
Stop recording a call unconditionally once it has been active this long. Useful
against peers that keep sending RTP long after the call ended.
.TP
.BI \-t " trigger" : action : parameter
Run something when a capture file is opened or closed. See
.B TRIGGERS
below.
.TP
.I expression
Any trailing arguments form a
.BR pcap-filter (7)
expression. It applies to signalling
.I and
media, so use it with care.
.SH FILE NAME TEMPLATES
The argument to
.B \-d
is passed through
.BR strftime (3),
using the call's start time, and then through three additional expansions:
.TP
.B %f
caller (the From user)
.TP
.B %t
callee (the To user)
.TP
.B %i
Call-ID
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
pcapsipdump \-i eth0 \\
  \-d '/var/spool/pcapsipdump/%Y%m%d/%H/%Y%m%d\-%H%M%S\-%f\-%t\-%i.pcap'
.fi
.RE
.SH TRIGGERS
.BI \-t " when" : action : parameter
hooks into the capture lifecycle.
.I when
is
.B open
or
.BR close ;
.I parameter
goes through the same expansion as
.BR \-d .
.TP
.BI mv: directory
Move the finished file elsewhere, using
.BR /bin/mv .
.TP
.BI exec:\(dq /bin/prog\ args \(dq
Fork and execute a program.
.TP
.BI sh:\(dq "shell code" \(dq
Fork and run
.B /bin/sh \-c
on the given code.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/default/pcapsipdump
Configuration read by the systemd units on Debian and Ubuntu.
.TP
.I /etc/sysconfig/pcapsipdump
The same, on RHEL-like distributions.
.TP
.I /var/spool/pcapsipdump
Default capture directory.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/pcapsipdump.service
The capture daemon. Not enabled on install; configure
.B DEVICE
first.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/pcapsipdump-cleanup.timer
Deletes captures older than
.B RETENTION
days, once a day.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success or after
.BR \-h / \-V ,
and non-zero when the arguments are invalid or the capture cannot be started.
.SH EXAMPLES
Split a capture somebody handed you:
.PP
.RS
.B pcapsipdump \-r bulk.pcap \-d /tmp/calls
.RE
.PP
Record only calls involving a German number, with RTP but not RTCP:
.PP
.RS
.B pcapsipdump \-i eth0 \-d /var/spool/pcapsipdump \-R rtp \-n '^\e+49'
.RE
.PP
Hand every finished call to a post-processing script:
.PP
.RS
.B pcapsipdump \-i eth0 \-d /var/spool/pcapsipdump \-t 'close:exec:/usr/local/bin/archive\-call.sh %i'
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tcpdump (8),
.BR pcap-filter (7),
.BR regex (7),
.BR strftime (3)
.PP
